# Why might you work less when your wage rises?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How does the labour–leisure choice work? Why can the labour supply curve bend backwards?"
next: {"title":"Why save at all?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/80-why-save-at-all.cast"}

## Kept poor to keep working
sources:
    - id: young1771
      title: The Farmer's Tour through the East of England, vol. 4, p. 361
      authors: Arthur Young
      year: 1771
      finding: '''Every one but an idiot knows, that the lower classes must be kept poor, or they will never be industrious.'''

    scratch quote_card cites ["young1771"] work ["“Every one but an idiot knows,","that the lower classes must be kept poor,","or they will never be industrious.”"] x 500 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text quote_by "Arthur Young, 1771" below quote_card gap 18 #8f887c font_size 26 hidden true
    text week "paid more → Mon–Thu at work, Fri–Sat off" #b5482e x 500 y 250 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Why might you work less when your wage rises?"

Why might you work less when your wage rises? Two and a half centuries ago, plenty of writers and employers were sure you would.
    draw quote_card_box quote_card_line_1 quote_card_line_2 quote_card_line_3

In 1771 the farming writer Arthur Young put it bluntly.
    draw quote_by

B: 'Every one but an idiot knows, that the lower classes must be kept poor, or they will never be industrious.'
    highlight quote_card effect underline

The thinking of the day went like this: pay a lab (@draw week@)ourer more, and he earns his week's bread by Thursday, then takes the rest of the week off.

    pause 0.3

B: Sounds like a convenient excuse for low pay. Surely a better wage makes people work more?
    fade quote_card quote_by week to 0.35

    quiz "Your hourly pay rises by half tomorrow, for good. Over the next year, you would work…" judge false
        intro: Test your own instinct first.
        right: People give all three answers, and each can be right.
        * More hours
        * About the same
        * Fewer hours

    erase quote_card quote_by week

Here's why all three answers can be right. A raise pulls on your hours in two directions at once.
    arrow axis #8f887c from.x 80 from.y 300 to.x 920 to.y 300
    text axis_name "hours worked" #3d3833 x 500 y 250 font_size 34
    text fewer "fewer" #8f887c x 110 y 255 font_size 30
    text more "more" #8f887c x 890 y 255 font_size 30
    icon marker size 120 x 500 y 370 or ["standing person"] icon_look picture of person

B: One pull: every hour at work now pays more, so every hour o (@move marker by [110,0] duration 1.2@)ff costs you more. That says work more.
    arrow pull_more #2f6b8f from.x 580 from.y 470 to.x 900 to.y 470 style.stroke_width 5
    text pull_more_label "each hour pays more" #2f6b8f x 740 y 525 font_size 36

The other: you're richer already. The same hours b (@move marker by [-110,0] duration 1.2@)uy more, so you can afford to take some time back.
    arrow pull_less #b5482e from.x 420 from.y 470 to.x 100 to.y 470 style.stroke_width 5
    text pull_less_label "you're richer" #b5482e x 260 y 525 font_size 36

    pause 0.3

Young bet on the second pull. To see which o (@highlight marker effect pulse@)ne wins, we need a picture of a working day.
    text which "which wins?" #3d3833 x 500 y 610 font_size 44

## Sofie's day
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 16
    price_x: 1
    price_y: 0.05
    preference: balanced
    curves: 2
    x_good: Hours off
    y_good: Dollars a day
    x_max: 18
    y_max: 520
    axis_numbers: true
    labels: false

    text sofie30 "Sofie" at.data [9.4,262] font_size 28 hidden true
    text sofie20 "Sofie" at.data [9.6,175] font_size 28 hidden true
    scale guess x 560 y 470 width 380 label "hours at work, at $30" unit h min 0 max 16 value 8 ticks 8 hidden true
    text read20 "$20: 8 h at work, $160" #8f887c x 800 y 655 font_size 30 hidden true
    text read30 "$30: 8 h at work, $240" #3d3833 x 800 y 655 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow pull_sub #2f6b8f from.data [8,195] to.data [6.2,195] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text pull_sub_label "← substitution (dearer time): −1.5 h off" #2f6b8f at.data [12.2,410] font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow pull_inc #b5482e from.data [6.2,155] to.data [8,155] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text pull_inc_label "→ income (richer): +1.5 h off" #b5482e at.data [12.2,365] font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch m top-right work [{"tex":"c = w\\,(16 - \\ell)"},{"tex":"U = c\\cdot\\ell \\;\\Rightarrow\\; w\\ell = \\tfrac12 \\cdot 16w"},{"tex":"w\\ell = 8w \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\ell = 8"}] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Sofie's day"

B: Picture one working day, with illustrative numbers. Sofie welds at the Harbourtown boatyard, and she has sixteen waking hours.
    draw axes

B: Hours off run along the bottom, dollars up the side.
    highlight axes

B: Each hour she gives up earns her twenty dollars, so her line runs from sixteen hours off and no pay, up to three hundred and twenty dollars.
    draw budget_line

So the wage is the slope of that line. It's the price of an hour off.
    highlight budget_line

B: Exactly. Her curves show what she likes, and her best point is eight hours off, eight at work:
    draw ic_1 ic_2

B: a hundred and sixty dollars.
    draw optimum sofie20 guide_x guide_y read20

    pause 0.3

    draw guess

    ask "How many hours will Sofie work at $30 an hour? Click the line." on guess store g default "11"
        intro: Now the boatyard raises her pay to thirty dollars an hour.
        right: Eight, the same as before.
        wrong: You said {g}. She works eight, the same as before.

    erase guess

    erase sofie20

    keep budget_line

B: Her line turns on its sixteen-hour end. A full day would now pay four hundred and eighty.
    animate price_y 0.0333333 duration 3

    erase read20

And she still works eight hours, now for two hundred and forty dollars.
    draw read30 sofie30

B: Both pulls are there; economists call them the substitution effect and the income effect. Each hour off now costs thirty dollars, which on its own would pull her to about an hour and a half more work.
    draw pull_sub pull_sub_label

And she's richer, which on its own would pull her an hour and a half back. For her, the two cancel to the minute.
    draw pull_inc pull_inc_label

    pause 0.4

    erase pull_sub pull_sub_label pull_inc pull_inc_label read30 budget_line_ghost sofie30

B: Try it: drag the top end of her line to raise or cut her wage, and watch her dot.
    explore

Straight up and down. With tastes like hers, the wage changes her pay, never her hours.
    highlight optimum guide_x

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store mq default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

Her pay is the wage times the hours she works: c equals w times sixteen minus her hours off.
    draw m_box m_line_1

With these balanced tastes she spends half of her day's full value, sixteen w, on time off.
    draw m_line_2

So w times her hours off is eight w. The w cancels: eight hours off, at any wage.
    draw m_line_3

    erase m

@after

    erase mathq

## The curve that bends back
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 5
      - 15
      - 25
      - 35
      - 45
      - 55
      - 65
      - 80
    series:
      - {name: hours, values: [6.1, 8.9, 9.5, 9.2, 8.5, 7.6, 6.7, 5.4]}
      - {name: after a big win, values: [4.8, 7.6, 8.2, 7.9, 7.2, 6.3, 5.4, 4.1]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 12
    xlim:
      - 0
      - 85
    x_label: Wage, $ an hour
    y_label: Hours worked a day
    smooth: true
    label_size: 26

    scratch calc work ["$1 more an hour","× 9.5 hours","= $9.50 a day"] x 330 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The curve that bends back"

Sofie's two pulls cancel exactly. Most people's don't, and which one wins can change as the wage climbs.
    draw axes

B: So let's draw a typical worker, with illustrative numbers: the wage along the bottom, hours a day up the side.
    point at.data [45,0] gesture underline

    ask "Your turn: draw how her hours go as the wage keeps rising, all the way to $80 an hour." on line_1 from 35 store g
        intro: At low wages a raise mostly makes work worth it. At five dollars an hour she works about six hours; at twenty-five, about nine and a half.
        right: Close: you were {g.pct} off on average.
        wrong: You were {g.pct} off on average. Her hours turn down.

It turns down. Past a point, each raise buys her more time off, not more work.
    highlight line_1

    pause 0.4

B: But why would the turn come up there, and not at the start?
    point at.data [25,9.5] gesture circle

Because the income pull grows with the hours you already work.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

A dollar more an hour, on nine and a half hours a day, is nine dollars fifty a day before she changes anything.
    draw calc_line_2 calc_line_3

    erase calc

B: And once the bills are paid, an extra free evening is worth more to most of us than one more thing to buy.
    point at.data [60,7.2] gesture tap

Economists call this shape the backward-bending labour supply curve.
    text bend "backward-bending" #b5482e at.data [66,8.4] font_size 32

    pause 0.3

B: And a big lottery win? Nothing about the price of an hour changes.
    highlight line_1

Then only the income pull works: fewer hours at every wage, and the whole curve drops.
    draw line_2

## What the winners did
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - before
      - after a 1M kronor win
    values:
      - 100
      - 94.5
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    y_label: yearly pay, before = 100
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 170
      w: 480
      h: 450
    label_size: 26
sources:
    - id: cesarini2017
      title: 'The Effect of Wealth on Individual and Household Labor Supply: Evidence from Swedish Lotteries'
      authors: David Cesarini, Erik Lindqvist, Matthew J. Notowidigdo and Robert Östling
      year: 2017
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20151589
      finding: 'Analysis sample of 249,278 Swedish lottery players aged 21–64 (J-PAL summary; about 2.5 million in the full lottery populations), pretax earnings fall by about 1.1% of the prize per year, immediately and for more than ten years: 1 million SEK (about $140,000) cuts annual earnings by about 11,000 SEK, 5.5% of average earnings; most of the response is fewer hours rather than leaving work.'
    - id: imbens2001
      title: 'Estimating the Effect of Unearned Income on Labor Earnings, Savings, and Consumption: Evidence from a Survey of Lottery Players'
      authors: Guido Imbens, Donald Rubin and Bruce Sacerdote
      year: 2001
      finding: Massachusetts Megabucks winners, paid in yearly instalments, reduced labour earnings by about 11 cents per dollar of yearly prize.
    - id: costa2000
      title: 'The Wage and the Length of the Work Day: From the 1890s to 1991'
      authors: Dora L. Costa
      year: 2000
      finding: In the 1890s the lowest-paid US workers worked the longest days and labour supply was strongly backward-bending; by 1991 the highest paid worked the longest days.

    scratch mass cites ["imbens2001"] work ["Massachusetts:","11¢ less pay","per $1 won"] x 790 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch costa cites ["costa2000"] work ["1890s: lowest paid work longest","1991: best paid work longest"] x 500 y 480 font_size 48 hidden true
    text sure1 "windfalls cut work a little: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sure2 "does the curve bend back today? contested" #b5482e x 500 y 235 font_size 36 hidden true
    cards pulls check each hidden true
        Only the income pull: An inheritance
        Both pulls: A pay rise
        Only the income pull: A lottery prize
        Both pulls: A cut in the tax on wages
        Only the income pull: Child benefit for every parent
        Both pulls: Time-and-a-half for overtime
    text swe "Swedish lottery players" #8f887c cites ["cesarini2017"] x 790 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text fin1 "a raise = dearer time + more money" x 500 y 450 font_size 46 hidden true
    text fin2 "the second pull: real, but gentle" #b5482e x 500 y 360 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "What the winners did"

B: So the curve can bend back. Does it, for real people? The cleanest test of the income pull is money that falls from the sky.
    draw axes

In Sweden, lottery prizes land on players at random, and economists followed hundreds of thousands of players through their tax records.
    draw bar_1 swe

    quiz "A Swede wins a million kronor, about $140,000. What happens to her yearly pay over the next ten years?" judge false
        right: Watch the second bar.
        * She quits work
        * It falls by about half
        * It falls by about 5%
        * It doesn't change

About five and a half percent: some eleven thousand kronor a year, year after year, for more than ten years.
    draw bar_2

More through fewer hours than through quitting. In Massachusetts, winners paid in yearly instalments earned about eleven cents less for each prize dollar.
    draw mass_box mass_line_1 mass_line_2 mass_line_3

B: So the income pull is real, but gentle. Young's labourer who stops on Thursday looks overdrawn.
    highlight bar_2

    erase mass axes bar_1 bar_2 swe

History has a twist, though. In America in the 1890s, the economic historian Dora Costa found, the lowest paid worked the longest days.
    draw costa_box costa_line_1

B: And by 1991 it had flipped: the best paid worked the longest.
    draw costa_line_2

So the hours of the low paid once looked like the top of a curve that bends back.
    highlight costa_line_1

Today's top earners look like the bottom of one that still climbs.
    highlight costa_line_2

B: Or something else is going on: careers, bonuses, work some people simply enjoy.
    fade costa to 0.4

How sure are we? That a windfall cuts work a little: mostly agreed.
    draw sure1

Whether today's cu (@draw sure2@)rve bends back: contested.

    pause 0.5

    erase costa

    quiz "A country starts paying every adult $1,000 a month, whatever they do. On theory alone, hours worked will…" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Fall a little: the payment makes people richer but leaves the pay for an hour unchanged, so only the income pull works.
        wrong: It leaves what an hour pays unchanged, so only the income pull works: here, fall a little.
        * rise
        * stay exactly the same
        + fall a little

    erase sure1 sure2

    draw pulls

    ask "Which pulls does each change set off? Sort each card." on pulls store f stage own voice b
        right: All six: anything that changes the pay for an hour sets off both pulls.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: here, both pulls for anything that changes the pay for an hour.

    erase pulls

    text surg "surgeon keeps: $100 → $150 an hour" x 500 y 420 font_size 40

    quiz "A tax cut lets a surgeon keep $150 of each $200 hour instead of $100. Can we be sure she will work more?"
        right: No: an hour off now costs her more, but she is also richer, and the two pulls fight.
        wrong: A raise after tax is still a raise, so both pulls work: here, no, she's richer too, so the pulls fight.
        * Yes, each hour pays more
        + No: she's richer too, so the pulls fight
        * No: taxes never change hours

    erase surg

So a raise can make you work less, because it makes you richer as well as making your time dearer. Arthur Yo (@draw fin2@)ung had the logic; the winners show the pull is gentler than he thought.
    draw fin1

